{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a major Christian leader in the Levant during the 12th century he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He became the Count of Tripoli after his father was murdered in 1152. Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a key figure in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin. \nThe *Early Life* of Raymond III is not well documented but it is known that he was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna. His father was a member of the House of Toulouse and his mother was a member of the House of Rethel. \nThe *Reign* of Raymond III was marked by conflict with the Muslim forces of Nur ad-Din and Saladin. In 1164 he was captured by the Muslims while fighting in the Battle of Harim but was later released. \nThe *Battle of Hattin* was a major defeat for the Christian forces and Raymond III played a significant role in the events leading up to the battle. He was a key advisor to King Guy of Lusignan and urged him to avoid battle with Saladin but his advice was ignored. \nThe *Later Life* of Raymond III is not well documented but it is known that he died in 1187. He was succeeded as Count of Tripoli by his nephew Raymond IV. \nThe *Legacy* of Raymond III is still debated among historians today. Some view him as a wise and experienced leader who was unable to prevent the defeat at Hattin while others view him as a coward who was responsible for the defeat. \nThe *Family* of Raymond III was a significant part of the nobility in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His mother was a member of the House of Rethel and his father was a member of the House of Toulouse. \nThe *Relationships* of Raymond III with other leaders in the Kingdom of Jerusalem were complex and often contentious. He was a key advisor to King Guy of Lusignan but the two men had significant disagreements. \nThe *Military Campaigns* of Raymond III were marked by conflict with the Muslim forces of Nur ad-Din and Saladin. He fought in several battles including the Battle of Harim and the Battle of Hattin. \nThe *Diplomatic Efforts* of Raymond III were significant and he played a key role in the negotiations between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Byzantine Empire. He also negotiated with the Muslim forces of Nur ad-Din and Saladin in an attempt to prevent conflict.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.9802276912960224e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0002613918040879071}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.00021705655672121793}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.3966996371746063}, {"token": " major", "logprob": -1.2672427892684937}, {"token": " Christian", "logprob": -1.371418833732605}, {"token": " leader", "logprob": -0.05213543400168419}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.018166350200772285}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -7.152531907195225e-06}, {"token": " Lev", "logprob": -0.6329652070999146}, {"token": "ant", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -5.94836674281396e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0003150205302517861}, {"token": "12", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.3241376578807831}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.13573408126831055}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0179595947265625}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.03444470092654228}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -6.9141146923357155e-06}, {"token": " II", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.005114566534757614}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "log